{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And the children struggled within her.</b> This pregnancy was unusual and different from the rest of women's pregnancies, for it is not the way of women that twins should struggle and quarrel in the womb, whereas these were struggling within her, causing her great suffering. And from the fact that they were struggling and fighting in the womb at the beginning of their formation, this was a hint and sign of what was destined to be — that they would stand against each other as haters, combatants. This is what the Holy One, blessed be He, said to her through the message of 'שני גוים בבטנך' — 'two nations are in your womb': that is, do not fear this, for this struggling is because of the two children that are in your womb, from whom two nations are destined to emerge, one against the other." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'שני גוים בבטנך' — do not read גוים (nations) but גיים (proud ones); this refers to Rabbi and Antoninus, who never lacked from their table neither lettuce nor radish, neither in the summer days nor in the rainy days. And although we find that Rabbi said at the hour of his passing, 'I did not enjoy even with my little finger,' the truth was that he afflicted himself in his own body and did not enjoy any worldly desires at all, except out of the most extreme necessity; but the array of foods and delicacies was for his attendants and those who ate at his table. Antoninus was a great king, of the seed of Esau, and he would study Torah in secret before our holy Rabbi, and he was afraid lest his servants and the men of his kingdom would notice. And they, our Rabbis of blessed memory, expounded in tractate Avodah Zarah (10b): there was a cave that went underground from his house to the house of Rabbi; every day he would bring two servants with him — one would kill [anyone] at the door of his house, and one would kill [anyone] at the door of Rabbi. He said to him: 'At the hour I come, let no one be found before you.' One day he found Rabbi Ḥanina bar Ḥama standing before him. He said to him: 'Did I not tell you that no one should be found before you?' He said to him: 'This is not a person [דלאו אינש הוא].' He said to him: 'If so, go and send me that man who was standing at the door, and let him come.' Rabbi Ḥanina went and found him slain. He said: 'What shall I do? If I tell him he is slain — one does not respond to a botched matter; if I leave him and go — I would be treating the kingdom with contempt.' He prayed for mercy over him and revived him and sent him. He said to him: 'I know that even the least among you revives the dead; yet at the hour I come, let no one be found before you.' Every day he would serve him: feed him, give him drink. When Rabbi wished to ascend to his bed, he would bend before the bed and say: 'Ascend upon your bed.' He said to him: 'It is not the way of the world to degrade the kingdom this much!' He said to him: 'Would that I might be a spread beneath you in the World to Come!' He said to him: 'Will I come to the World to Come?' He said to him: 'Yes.' He said to him: 'But it is written (Obadiah 1:18) ״ולא יהיה שריד לבית עשו״ — and there shall be no survivor of the house of Esau!' He said to him: '[That applies] to one who does the deed of Esau.' He said to him: 'But it is written (Ezekiel 32:29) ״שמה אדום מלכיה ושריה״ — there is Edom, her kings and her princes!' He said to him: '״מלכיה״ — her kings, and not all her kings; ״שריה״ — her princes, and not all her princes' — excluding Antoninus ben Asuiros, and excluding Keti'a bar Shalom.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:15:10.994137553Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}